1.3 Mathematical statements and negation
    Statement:
        Def: A statement is a sentence that has a definite state of being either true or false
            Ex. 2 + 5 = 7
                if x = 3, then x^2 = 9
        
        on the other hand, if a sentence that cant easily be determined by true or false, 
        it won't be a statement
            Ex. x^2 = 9
                x^2 >= 0 note: this is not a statement, since we didn't point out what the domain it has,
                               so that we cant determine it is correct or not.
                x = 3

    Negation:
        Def: if A is a statement. Then the negation of A, denoted by ¬A. That is, if the A is 
            true statement, then ¬A is the false statement. ¬A always means the oppsite truth value.

1.4 Quantifiers and Quantified statements
    
    Open sentence:
        A sentence that contains a bariable, where the truth of the sentence is determined by the value of 
        the varialbe chosen from the domain of the variable. (and technically, the question of truth has no meaning
        unless a value for the variable is specified)

        note: open statement is NOT a statement
    
    Quantified statements:
        A quantified statement is obtained by attaching a quantifier to an open statement P(x), specifying how many
        values of the variable make the statement true

        A quantified statement contains four parts:
            - a quantifier (universal, or existential)
            - a variable
            - a domain
            - an open sentence involving the variable

    Two types of quantified statements:
        suppose taht we are given S and an open sentence P(x). We consider two types of quantified statements:

        1. A universally quantified statement is of the form:
            ∀ x ∈ S, P(x)
            "For all x in S, P(x)"
            ∀ is quantifier, x is variable, s is domain, p(x) is open sentence

        2. An existentially quantified statement is of the form:
            ∃ x ∈ S, P(x)
            "There exists an x in S such that P(x)"
            ∃ is quantifier, x is variable, s is domain, p(x) is open sentence

    Negation of Quantifiers:
        Quantified statement         True                        False
            ∀ x ∈ S, P(x)       when P(x) is true        when P(x) is false for 
                                for every x ∈ S are      at least one x ∈ S is false
                                true
            ∃ x ∈ S, P(x)       when P(x) is true for   when P(x) is false for
                                for at least one x ∈ S  every x ∈ S are false 
                                is true
        we can write logical equivalence with "≡" sign, it means logically equal
            Ex. ¬(∀x ∈S, P(x)) ≡ (∃x ∈S, ¬ P(x))

    How to get the negation of a quantified statement:
        1. reverse the quantifier
        2. reverse the open sentence 

1.5 Nested Quantifiers:
    The quantifier in a statement containing more than one quantifier are called nested quantifier
        Ex. ∀ s ∈ R, ∃ t ∈ R, t > s
            read as "for all real number s, there exists at least a real number t, which t > s"
    
    when dealing with the nested quantified statement, must be vary careful about the left to right order
    it may change the truth of the statement
    based on this, there will be 3 situations:

    case 1. two different quantifier:   
        suppose that X and Y are any sets, and Q(x, y) is any open sentence
            ∀ x ∈ X, ∃ y ∈ Y, Q(x, y) and ∃ y ∈ Y, ∀ x ∈ X, Q(x, y)
        are not logically equivalent

    case 2. two universal quantifier:
        suppose that X and Y are any sets, and Q(x, y) is any open sentence
            ∀ x ∈ X, ∀ y ∈ Y, Q(x, y) and ∀ y ∈ Y, ∀ x ∈ X, Q(x, y)
        are logically equivalent

    case 3. two existential quantifier:
        suppose that X and Y are any sets, and Q(x, y) is any open sentence
            ∃ x ∈ X, ∃ y ∈ Y, Q(x, y) and ∃ y ∈ Y, ∃ x ∈ X, Q(x, y)
        are logically equivalent

    for number of quantifier is more than 2, we can analysis them with layers:
        Ex. ∃ x ∈ X, ∀ y ∈ Y, ∃ z ∈ Z, R(x, y, z)
            -> ∃ x ∈ X, (∀ y ∈ Y, (∃ z ∈ Z, R(x, y, z)))
                {∃ x ∈ X, P(x),
                {where P(x) is ∀ y ∈ Y, Q(x, y),
                {where Q(x, y) is ∃ z ∈ Z, R(x, y, z)
    
    Negation of Nested Quantifiers:
        For getting the negation of nested quantifier, just find the nagation layer by layer
            Ex.
                ¬(∃ x ∈ X, P(x)) ≡ (∀ x ∈ X, ¬P(x))
            -> where ¬P(x) ≡ ¬(∀ y ∈ Y, Q(x, y)) ≡ (∃ y ∈ Y, ¬ Q(x, y))
            -> where ¬Q(x, y) ≡ ¬(∃ z ∈ Z, R(x, y, z)) ≡ (∀z ∈Z, ¬ R(x, y, z))

            putting all sub-layer nagation together:
                we get the final result: 
                   ¬(∃x ∈X, ∀y ∈Y, ∃z ∈Z, R(x, y, z)) ≡ (∀x ∈X, ∃y ∈Y, ∀z ∈Z, ¬ R(x, y, z))

                